<BaseLayout
  title="Waist-to-Height Ratio Calculator – WHtR Tool | Nawtch"
  description="Free waist-to-height ratio calculator using NICE's guideline thresholds. A companion to BMI that accounts for where you carry weight, not just how much."
  canonicalPath="/calculators/waist-to-height/"
  bodyClass="calculators"
>
  <header>
    <div class="header-left">
      <h1>Waist-to-Height Ratio Calculator</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">See how your waist compares to your height — a companion to BMI that accounts for fat distribution.</p>
    </div>
    <a class="ghost-btn" href="/calculators/">← Back to Calculators</a>
  </header>

  <p class="field-hint" style="margin:-4px 0 14px;">Already have your BMI? This pairs well with our <a href="/calculators/bmi/">BMI Calculator</a> for a fuller picture — BMI looks at overall size, this looks at where you carry weight.</p>

  <div id="calc-whtr"></div>

  <div id="calc-whtr-explain" class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">How We Calculate This</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        Waist-to-height ratio (WHtR) is simply <strong>waist circumference ÷ height</strong>, measured in the
        same unit (both inches or both cm). It's a proxy for central adiposity — fat carried around the
        abdomen — which tracks more closely with cardiometabolic risk than total body size alone.
        <br /><br />
        We use the classification bands from the UK's National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE)
        guideline NG246 (2025), recommendation 1.9.14:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>0.40–0.49</strong> — Healthy central adiposity, no increased health risk</li>
        <li><strong>0.50–0.59</strong> — Increased central adiposity, increased health risk</li>
        <li><strong>0.60 or more</strong> — High central adiposity, further increased health risk</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="settings-desc">
        NICE states these bands can be used for adults with a <strong>BMI under 35 kg/m²</strong>, of both
        sexes and all ethnicities, including adults with high muscle mass — above that BMI, other assessment
        methods take over, since the calculator becomes less useful at that point. NICE also gives a
        plain-language version of the same threshold, worth remembering on its own: try to keep your waist
        circumference to <strong>less than half your height</strong> (a ratio under 0.5).
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to measure your waist:</strong> find the bottom of your ribs and the top of your hip
        bone, then wrap the tape measure around your waist midway between the two — roughly level with your
        belly button. Keep the tape snug against the skin without compressing it, and breathe out naturally
        before reading the measurement. Where you place the tape affects the result more than most people
        expect, so measuring the same spot the same way each time matters more than any single reading.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Worth knowing:</strong> this is a screening tool, not a diagnosis — it's meant to inform a
        conversation with a doctor or dietitian, not replace one. NICE's classification bands here are
        specifically for adults; children and young people are assessed under a separate NICE recommendation
        with age-specific context, so this calculator (like our other calculators) is built for adult use.
        The ratio also assumes a fairly stable standing height and a waist measurement that reflects body
        composition rather than something else changing it — it isn't designed for use during pregnancy, or
        for anyone whose height has been significantly altered by a spinal or growth-related condition, since
        the underlying assumption (waist tracks fat, height is stable) doesn't hold as well in those cases.
      </p>
      <strong>Primary evidence:</strong> Ashwell, M., Gunn, P., &amp; Gibson, S. (2012). <em>Waist-to-height
      ratio is a better screening tool than waist circumference and BMI for adult cardiometabolic risk
      factors: systematic review and meta-analysis.</em> Obesity Reviews, 13(3), 275–286. A meta-analysis of
      31 studies and more than 300,000 adults found WHtR discriminated cardiometabolic risk factors (diabetes,
      hypertension, cardiovascular disease) 4–5% better than BMI, and better than waist circumference alone.
      <br /><br />
      <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22106927/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the meta-analysis →</a>
      <br /><br />
      <strong>Guideline:</strong> National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (2025). <em>NG246:
      Overweight and obesity management.</em> Recommendations 1.9.14–1.9.15.
      <br /><br />
      <a href="https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng246/chapter/Identifying-and-assessing-overweight-obesity-and-central-adiposity" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the NICE guideline →</a>
      <br /><br />
      <strong>Recent supporting study:</strong> Mendes, T.B., Bittencourt, M.S., et al. (2025). <em>Waist-to-height
      ratio and coronary artery calcium incidence: the Brazilian Longitudinal Study of Adult Health
      (ELSA-Brasil).</em> The Lancet Regional Health – Americas. Following 2,721 adults with no cardiovascular
      disease at baseline for over 5 years, WHtR remained a significant predictor of new coronary artery
      calcium after adjusting for standard risk factors (age, sex, smoking, exercise, diabetes, hypertension,
      cholesterol) — BMI and waist circumference alone did not.
      <br /><br />
      <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41541766/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the study →</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <script src="../../../islands/vanilla/calculators/waist-to-height/waist-to-height.js"></script>
</BaseLayout>
